00:44:49.120 --> 00:44:56.800
you need to have the math background of 3d
matrices and transformations. Since our computer

00:44:56.800 --> 00:45:05.680
screen is a two dimensional, it cannot directly
display 3d Objects. To view 3d objects on a 2d

00:45:05.680 --> 00:45:15.360
screen, you have to project your object from
2d to 3d, that will involve a series of coordinate

00:45:15.360 --> 00:45:25.040
transformations. From here you can see that we
define 3d object in the object coordinate system

00:45:25.040 --> 00:45:33.920
here. We then perform various transformations on
the object, including scaling, translation and

00:45:33.920 --> 00:45:41.440
rotation. We call this transformation as a model
transform. After this transformation, we convert

00:45:41.440 --> 00:45:49.920
an object in the object coordinates into
the object in the world space. Next, the view

00:45:49.920 --> 00:45:59.600
transformation locates the viewer in World space
and transform our 3d object into camera space

00:45:59.633 --> 00:46:08.353
that also called the eye coordinates. The purpose
of the projection transform here is to define a view

00:46:08.880 --> 00:46:16.240
volume called  view frustum, right here.
This is a view frustum. This is used in two

00:46:16.240 --> 00:46:24.720
ways: it determines how an object is a project
on the screen. It also defines which portions

00:46:24.720 --> 00:46:32.720
of the object are clipped out of the final image.
That is only the portion inside this frustum

00:46:32.720 --> 00:46:41.280
will be kept and anything outside of this frustum
will be clipped out. Finally, we use the view

00:46:41.280 --> 00:46:51.040
port transform to convert the clip coordinates
into the normalized device coordinates. In WebGPU.

00:46:51.040 --> 00:46:58.800
The viewport transform here is automatically
performed in the shader code. Please note

00:46:58.800 --> 00:47:05.520
that like a WebGL, the WebGPU API
does not provide any functions for

00:47:06.080 --> 00:47:14.640
working with transformation. In this project, we
will use a JavaScript package called GL matrix to

00:47:14.640 --> 00:47:23.200
