01:09:08.240 --> 01:09:16.320
So now with how completed this
project. In the previous several projects,

01:09:16.320 --> 01:09:25.680
I explained how to create some 2d and 3d objects.
However, when rendering these graphics objects with

01:09:25.680 --> 01:09:33.040
solid colors, you may find that the image
looks flat and it is failed to illustrate

01:09:33.040 --> 01:09:41.600
the 3d nature of the objects. This is because we
neglect interaction between light and surface in

01:09:41.600 --> 01:09:50.160
our objects. Lighting helps provide visual effect to
a scene so that it looks more realistic. Lighting

01:09:50.160 --> 01:09:59.360
is one of the most important factors for creating
real world shaded 3d graphics objects. However,

01:09:59.360 --> 01:10:07.920
WebGPU does not provide much built-in
features on lighting. It just runs two functions:

01:10:07.920 --> 01:10:14.640
vertex and fragment shaders. If you want the
lighting effect in your 3d scene, you have

01:10:14.640 --> 01:10:22.480
to create the lighting model yourself. In this
project, I will build a simple lighting model in

01:10:22.480 --> 01:10:30.560
WebGPU and use it to simulate the light source.
Here you can download the source code used in this

01:10:30.560 --> 01:10:38.320
project from this GitHub repository link. Here
The specific commit version is used

01:10:38.320 --> 01:10:45.840
for this project. Here I will discuss three
types of light sources, ambient light,

01:10:45.840 --> 01:10:53.040
diffuse light, and specular light. The
ambient light is the light that illuminates

01:10:53.040 --> 01:11:01.120
all objects uniformly regardless of their location
or orientation. It is a global illumination

01:11:01.120 --> 01:11:08.560
in an environment. The diffuse light and
specular light reflection depends on the angle

01:11:08.560 --> 01:11:15.440
of the light to a surface. When the light hits
the surface., the diffuse reflection occurs in

01:11:15.440 --> 01:11:24.560
all directions, as shown here, while the specular
light reflection occurs in a single direction.

01:11:24.560 --> 01:11:35.040
as shown here. Here shows the light reflection on a
torus surface. On the left here, light is only from

01:11:35.040 --> 01:11:44.560
ambient light, you can see this torus looks very
flat. Here in the center shows light from both

01:11:44.560 --> 01:11:54.160
ambient plus the diffuse light. You can see
the 3d feature already shows here. On the right here,

01:11:54.160 --> 01:12:02.880
we have three light sources: ambient plus diffuse
and plus specular lights. You can see this

01:12:02.880 --> 01:12:10.240
torus looks more realistic. So, this is the
light model we want to build in this project.

01:12:10.800 --> 01:12:17.760
The light reflection depends on the angle at which
the light hits the surface. The angle is essential

01:12:17.760 --> 01:12:24.640
to the diffuse light and the specular light.
This angle is always associated with surface

01:12:24.640 --> 01:12:31.920
normal that is a perpendicular to the surface.
To build the light model, we need to calculate the

01:12:31.920 --> 01:12:39.040
surface normal based on the direction in which
the surface is facing. Since the surface of 3d

01:12:39.040 --> 01:12:46.240
object can be curved, it can face different
directions at different vertex. So,

01:12:46.240 --> 01:12:54.480
a normal vector is usually different for different
vertex that is normal vector is always associated

01:12:54.480 --> 01:13:03.360
with a particular point on the surface. Here shows
normal vector for 3d cube. Here, the front face is

01:13:03.360 --> 01:13:12.240
pointing towards the screen. So its normal
vector is 001. The right face is facing

01:13:12.240 --> 01:13:21.600
direction to the right. So its normal is 1
00. And the top face is facing towards the top. So,

01:13:21.600 --> 01:13:31.840
its normal vector is 010. So, similarly, you
can specify the normal vectors for the back

01:13:32.480 --> 01:13:39.600
left and the bottom faces. Note that the
length of the normal vectors should be always

01:13:40.240 --> 01:13:47.360
one, that is, we need to normalize the
normal vectors. For a general quadrilateral A B

01:13:47.360 --> 01:13:57.600
C, D, its 4 vertices may not in the same plane,
we can divide it into two triangles, A, B,

01:13:57.600 --> 01:14:06.480
C and C D A. We can calculate its surface
normal in two steps. First, we calculate the

01:14:06.480 --> 01:14:13.040
weighted normal for each triangle, which is a
weighted by the area of that triangle.

01:14:13.040 --> 01:14:20.160
The triangle with larger area will get
more weight. As we know the area of a triangle

01:14:20.160 --> 01:14:29.120
is proportional to its cross product of two
sides. For example, here, for triangle A, B,

01:14:29.120 --> 01:14:38.080
C, we have a cross product B, C, and C-A,
b c and a c. Similarly for the triangle C

01:14:38.080 --> 01:14:47.280
D A, we have a cross product D A and A C. The next
step is to calculate the surface normal from the

01:14:47.280 --> 01:14:54.720
weighted triangle normals, that is, the surface
normal equals the sum of two weighted normals,

01:14:54.720 --> 01:15:02.880
and we can then normalize it. So we get this
quadrilateral normal equals normalize this

01:15:02.880 --> 01:15:10.000
two cross products. The sum of this one triangle
plus another one. So, we can combine these two

01:15:10.000 --> 01:15:18.800
terms to form one term equals cross product of
C A and D B. So, this means the surface normal

01:15:18.800 --> 01:15:28.480
for this general quadrilateral just equals
to the cross product of C, A, and d b. Just

01:15:28.480 --> 01:15:37.600
equal to the cross product of these two diagonal
vectors. We will use this formula to calculate

01:15:37.600 --> 01:15:45.360
the light model for our 3d surfaces. Once we
have calculated the normal vector, we can use it

01:15:45.360 --> 01:15:54.080
to compute the light intensity. For diffuse light,
its intensity is proportional to the cosine

01:15:54.080 --> 01:16:02.160
between the light vector here and a normal
vector N. You can see here the cosine alpha equals

01:16:02.160 --> 01:16:11.520
normalized L dot N here. If we define the Id, the
diffuse intensity, Kd the diffuse material

01:16:11.520 --> 01:16:20.960
property, so we can have this formula: the diffuse
intensity equals Kd times maximum cosine alpha and 0. Here

01:16:20.960 --> 01:16:29.680
the maximum is used to avoid the negative value of
the cosine alpha here, we only take the positive

01:16:29.680 --> 01:16:38.000
value. We can also add the ambient light to
the diffuse light intensity like this. Here Ia

01:16:39.120 --> 01:16:48.320
is ambient light intensity. So the total light intensity
is maxima Kd and here is the Ia. So we add

01:16:48.320 --> 01:16:56.240
the ambient intensity to this diffuse light like
this. For specular light, we have two models

01:16:56.240 --> 01:17:04.000
one is Phong model, this model is very simple. So
the Is, the intensity of the specular light, equals

01:17:04.000 --> 01:17:11.120
to Ks, Ks is speclar material property, and s
is the shininess of the surface or roughness,

01:17:11.120 --> 01:17:18.320
the V is the view direction, R is reflection
direction. One issue with Phong model is that

01:17:18.320 --> 01:17:26.560
the angle between the view direction and reflection
direction has to be less than 90 degrees in order

01:17:26.560 --> 01:17:34.640
for the specular Phong term to contribute. We can
use the Blinn-Phong model to address this issue.

01:17:35.600 --> 01:17:42.000
Blinn-Phong model uses a different set of
vectors for its computation, based on the half

01:17:42.000 --> 01:17:50.240
angle vector. Here's a half angle vector, you can see L 
is the light direction, N is normal vector, V is

01:17:50.240 --> 01:17:58.240
view direction, H is the half angle vector defined
L is the light direction plus the view direction. The

01:17:58.240 --> 01:18:06.240
view direction plus the light direction gives H
here. The formula in Blinn-Phong model becomes

01:18:06.800 --> 01:18:16.720
N dot H instead of V dot R here. So the
angle between this N and H is always less

01:18:16.720 --> 01:18:25.040
than 90 degrees. So this N dot H
always gives positive value. So we will use this

01:18:25.040 --> 01:18:32.960
Blinn-Phong mode in our calculation for the specular
light. Now let's go to the programming part.

01:18:32.960 --> 01:18:40.400
We started with Visual Studio Code and open
project five that we built in the last section.

01:18:41.280 --> 01:18:50.560
Here is the code for our project file. Now let's
implement the light model in the shader code. From

01:18:50.560 --> 01:18:59.520
src folder open the shader.wgsl. We need 
to replace its content with the new code.

01:19:00.880 --> 01:19:08.640
Here we first define the uniform structure
here that contains view-projection matrix,

01:19:08.640 --> 01:19:16.400
the model matrix and normal matrix. Here
the normal matrix is transformation matrix

01:19:16.400 --> 01:19:23.760
for the normal vector data. When applying a transform
to a surface, we need to derive the normal vector

01:19:23.760 --> 01:19:31.920
for the resulting surface from original normal
vectors. In order to transform the normal vector

01:19:31.920 --> 01:19:41.520
correctly, we don't simply multiply them by the
same matrix used to transform our object, but we

01:19:41.520 --> 01:19:50.320
need to multiply them by the transpose of the
inverse of that matrix. Since the current WG

01:19:50.320 --> 01:19:59.040
SL version does not implement the matrix inverse
function yet, so we have to make this transpose

01:19:59.040 --> 01:20:07.040
of the inverse of the transform matrix in
TypeScript code, and then pass it as a uniform

01:20:07.040 --> 01:20:15.440
matrix as shown here, normal matrix. We then
define output structure that contains three

01:20:15.440 --> 01:20:23.120
variables: built-in position, the v-position
the position after some transformation, we call it the

01:20:23.120 --> 01:20:29.120
v-position and the v_normal, which is a normal
vector after transformation. In our vs

01:20:29.120 --> 01:20:37.680
main function, it contains two input arguments
one is vertex position called the pos and

01:20:37.680 --> 01:20:46.640
one is normal vecctor data. Inside this function, we only
perform the model transform on the position. This gives us

01:20:46.640 --> 01:20:54.800
v-position here because it is used in the light
calculation. The  v-normal is a result of normal

01:20:54.800 --> 01:21:02.880
vector data multiplied by the normal transform
matrix here. And the default position here

01:21:02.880 --> 01:21:12.640
is obtained by performing model-view and projection
transform on the vertex data. For the fragment shader

01:21:12.640 --> 01:21:20.000
we first introduce the uniform
structure called FragUniform that contains two

01:21:20.000 --> 01:21:28.240
variables: one is light position of light
vector, and the other one is eye position. Or

01:21:28.240 --> 01:21:36.720
view vector. It also has another structure called
LightUniform. Here contains parameters for our

01:21:37.280 --> 01:21:44.400
light model. Here's a light color and a specular
color. The specular light can have its own

01:21:44.400 --> 01:21:51.920
color, and the other parameters contain ambient
intensity, diffuse intensity, specular intensity

01:21:51.920 --> 01:21:59.520
and the specular shininess. Here the fs_main
function takes v_position and v_normal

01:21:59.520 --> 01:22:07.360
as inputs. Inside this function, we calculate
the light model. Here is the diffuse. Just use N dot

01:22:07.360 --> 01:22:15.440
L, the cosine alpha. For the specular light, we use Blinn
Phong model. This  is N dot H. The output color

01:22:15.440 --> 01:22:24.400
from fragment shader will be weighted, you can
see, by ambient, diffuse, and specular light.

01:22:24.400 --> 01:22:32.640
Its combination gives us the final color. So, this
is the shader we are going to use to calculate the

01:22:32.640 --> 01:22:41.920
light model. Now we can save this file and close
it. Next in the src folder, we will add a new type

01:22:41.920 --> 01:22:51.840
script file called light.ts. Add a new file
light.ts file. Here is the code for this file.

01:22:52.720 --> 01:23:00.480
Here we first create an interface named light
inputs that contains parameters used for

01:23:00.480 --> 01:23:07.920
calculating light model. This code also contains
a new function create-shape-with-light

01:23:07.920 --> 01:23:17.840
which has four input arguments: one is the vertex
data, normal data, and light input, and the is

01:23:17.840 --> 01:23:26.080
animation parameters. Here we set the default
light parameters if no parameters were passed,

01:23:26.080 --> 01:23:31.280
so we have some default values for
the light parameters. Next we create

01:23:31.280 --> 01:23:37.280
a vertex buffer and a normal buffer here.
You can see that the pipeline contains

01:23:37.280 --> 01:23:45.520
a buffers attribute here. It is an array that contains
two elements. The first element is the vertex

01:23:45.520 --> 01:23:52.960
data with the shader location being zero. The
second element is for the normal vector data

01:23:52.960 --> 01:23:59.280
with the shader location being one here. The
other part of the pipeline is the same to that

01:24:00.320 --> 01:24:09.280
we used in Project five. Next we create a uniform
data. Here we define normal matrix and a model

01:24:09.280 --> 01:24:18.960
matrix, and view, and view-projection matrices here. For
simplicity, here we set the eye position we get

01:24:18.960 --> 01:24:26.800
from the camera equals eye position. This means
the light vector is equal to the view vector. Here

01:24:26.800 --> 01:24:35.600
we then create three uniform buffers: one is vertex
uniform buffer, another one is a fragment uniform

01:24:35.600 --> 01:24:42.640
buffer, and finally it is light uniform buffer.
The vertex uniform buffer is used to store

01:24:42.640 --> 01:24:49.840
the model matrix, vew-projection matrix, and
a normal matrix. The fragment uniform buffer is used

01:24:50.640 --> 01:24:56.320
to store the eye position and light
position. And the light uniform buffer is used to

