01:45:00.320 --> 01:45:07.680
the colormap model. In this project I will
explain how to use this model to build simple 3d

01:45:07.680 --> 01:45:16.240
surfaces. Here is an example of a 3d Simple
surface created using a peaks function.

01:45:16.800 --> 01:45:26.000
Mathematically, a surface draws a Y function on the
surface for each x and z coordinates in a region

01:45:26.000 --> 01:45:34.720
of interest. For each x and z value pair the
simple 3d surface can have at the most one Y

01:45:35.360 --> 01:45:43.440
value. We can define a simple surface by the y
coordinates of point above a rectangular grid.

01:45:43.440 --> 01:45:51.360
In the x-z plane, the surface is formed by 
jointing adjacent points using straight lines.

01:45:51.360 --> 01:45:56.560
Typically, the surface is formed
using rectangular mesh grdids.

01:45:56.560 --> 01:46:03.840
However, WebGPU only provides
triangles as the basic units to represent

01:46:03.840 --> 01:46:12.240
any surface in 3d. In order to represent a surface
using traditional quadrilaterals, we need to write

01:46:12.240 --> 01:46:19.600
our own functions. You can download the source
code used in this project from this GitHub

01:46:19.600 --> 01:46:25.680
repository link. This specific commit
version is used for this project.

01:46:25.680 --> 01:46:33.600
Now start Visual Studio Code and open project
8 that we built in the last section.

01:46:34.160 --> 01:46:43.440
Here shows the code used in the project 8.
First we need to add a new typescript file

01:46:43.440 --> 01:46:54.080
called surface-data.ts to the src folder, SRC
folder. Add a new file called surface-data.ts

01:46:55.200 --> 01:47:09.200
We need to introduce vec3,  import vec3,
from gl-matrix library, and also need to import from

01:47:09.200 --> 01:47:15.680
colormap-data.ts file, introduce
addColors function. First, let

01:47:16.400 --> 01:47:24.160
add a utility function called normalize-point that
will be used to normalize the point to the range

01:47:24.160 --> 01:47:31.920
of minus one and a one. So, we need to add a
function normalize function. You can see here

01:47:31.920 --> 01:47:39.360
this function takes a 3d vec3 here
the points. x data range: X minimum, X

01:47:39.360 --> 01:47:46.720
maximum, y minimum, y maximum, z minimum,
z maximum, and also introduce a global scale,

01:47:46.720 --> 01:47:53.840
parameter that allows you to change the value
range for the x, y, and z coordinates. This will

01:47:53.840 --> 01:48:01.760
